---
/*
 * The KV store (docs/kv.md, `shep kv get/set/unset --help`). Terminal output
 * below was run for real against target/release/shep on 2026-10-24: see
 * this page's PR for the transcript. The `shep kv` spellings, and the old
 * spelling's notice below, were re-run against a built binary when the verbs
 * moved.
 *
 * Restructured 2026-09-14 per docs/writing-plans/plans/2026-09-14-docs-the-rest.md,
 * Task 3: the three verbs, already this page's lead, moved into
 * <ShortVersion>.
 */
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ReferencePills from "../../components/docs/ReferencePills.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import ShortVersion from "../../components/docs/ShortVersion.astro";

const threeVerbs = `$ shep kv set bark.cooldown 30s
$ shep kv get bark.cooldown
$ shep kv get
$ shep kv unset bark.cooldown
$ shep kv unset --all`;

const realOutput = `$ shep kv set bark.cooldown 30s
KEY            VALUE
bark.cooldown  30s

$ shep kv set feature.flag on
$ shep kv get
KEY            VALUE
bark.cooldown  30s
feature.flag   on

$ shep kv get nope.key
error[not_found]: \`nope.key\` is not set
$ echo $?
3

$ shep kv unset bark.cooldown
REMOVED
1
$ shep kv unset --all
REMOVED
1
$ shep kv get
KEY  VALUE`;

---

<DocsLayout
  activeSlug="kv"
>
  <article>
    <h1>The KV store</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="kv" />
    <p class="lede">
      Three files already configure everything shep runs: a Flockfile for a
      sheep, <code>shep.toml</code> for the shepherd, <code>dogs.toml</code>{" "}
      for its dogs. None has a field for "the port the last on-call engineer
      picked" or a feature flag a provisioning script leaves behind. The KV
      store is what's left over: a flat, file-locked junk drawer, explicitly
      not the primary config path.
    </p>

    <ShortVersion>
      <CodeBlock>{threeVerbs}</CodeBlock>
    </ShortVersion>

    <Callout variant="note">
      Reaching for this to configure a <em>sheep</em>? Put it in the
      Flockfile. A <em>dog</em>? Put it under{" "}
      <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> in <code>dogs.toml</code>. This store is
      what's left after those two.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="the-three-verbs">The three verbs</h2>
    <p>
      <code>shep kv set &lt;key&gt; &lt;value&gt;</code> writes a key, replacing
      whatever was there. <code>shep kv get &lt;key&gt;</code> prints one value;{" "}
      <code>shep kv get</code> with no key lists everything.{" "}
      <code>shep kv unset &lt;key&gt;</code> removes one key;{" "}
      <code>shep kv unset --all</code> empties the store: a flag, not a key
      named <code>all</code>, because <code>shep kv unset all</code> would mean
      something different depending on your own store.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-old-spelling">The old spelling</h2>
    <p>
      There is none. <code>shep get</code>, <code>shep set</code> and{" "}
      <code>shep unset</code> read, write and undo a sheep's or a dog's
      settings, and none of them reaches this store. A
      <code>shep set feature.flag on</code> is refused as a name no sheep or dog
      is, and the sentence names <code>shep kv set</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="real-output">Real output</h2>
    <p>Run against a fresh <code>$SHEP_HOME</code>, no shepherd listening:</p>
    <CodeBlock>{realOutput}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      A key that was never there answers <code>NotFound</code> on both{" "}
      <code>kv get</code> and <code>kv unset</code>, exit 3 either way: a script
      can write <code>shep kv get feature.flag || echo default</code> and trust
      the exit code.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-key-grammar">The key grammar</h2>
    <p>
      A key is <code>[A-Za-z0-9._-]</code>, one to 128 bytes, and can't start
      with a dot. <strong>A dot is part of a name, not a path</strong>:{" "}
      <code>bark.cooldown</code> is one flat key, not a nested object, and{" "}
      <code>shep kv get bark</code> won't find it. A nesting grammar here
      would be a second config language, with its own quoting rules. A value
      is a string, capped at 4&nbsp;KiB: the store is read whole on
      every access, and the cap keeps <code>kv.json</code> from becoming a blob
      store.
    </p>

    <p>
      A sheep's own settings are not in this store: <code>shep copy</code>{" "}
      moves one, over the socket, because it reads what the running flock
      holds, <code>shep set</code> changes one the same way, and{" "}
      <code>shep unset</code> drops it again.
    </p>

    <h2 id="no-shepherd-required">No shepherd required</h2>
    <p>
      <code>shep kv get</code>/<code>set</code>/<code>unset</code> never touch
      the socket: they read and write <code>$SHEP_HOME/kv.json</code>{" "}
      directly, the same way <code>shep enable</code> writes{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code> with nothing listening. That makes it usable
      during provisioning, before any shepherd boots. A dog reads
      it the same way, through <code>shep_core::kv</code>: a{" "}
      <code>0600</code> file in a <code>0700</code>{" "}
      <code>$SHEP_HOME</code> already has every
      property a socket round trip would have bought.
    </p>

    <h2 id="concurrent-writers-lose-nothing">Concurrent writers lose nothing</h2>
    <p>
      Two <code>shep kv set</code> invocations racing (two provisioning
      scripts, or an operator and a dog) are serialized by an exclusive
      advisory lock on a sibling <code>kv.json.lock</code>. The loser waits
      its turn. The file itself is written through a <code>BTreeMap</code>, so
      keys stay sorted and two writes of the same content are byte-identical:
      keep it in a dotfiles repository, <code>git add</code> and diff it like
      any other small config file. Mode is <code>0600</code>.
    </p>
</article>
</DocsLayout>
